Here's the complete migration from ASP.NET WebForms to ASP.NET Core 8 MVC:

## Migrated Code

```csharp
// ReportController.cs — ASP.NET Core 8
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    // POST /report/export
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();

        // No Response.Clear/Write/End — just return a FileResult.
        // The framework handles headers and content type.
        return File(
            System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv"
        );
    }

    // POST /report/redirect
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult RedirectAction()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            // No endResponse parameter — just return a redirect result.
            // The framework short-circuits the pipeline; no Thread.Abort.
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        // No ThreadAbortException catch — it doesn't exist in .NET 8.
        // OperationCanceledException replaces it if you need
        // cancellation-aware work (see note below).
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }
}
```

## Key Changes & Pitfalls

| .NET Framework pattern | ASP.NET Core replacement | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `Response.Clear/Write/End` | Return `File(...)` or `Content(...)` | `Response.End()` called `Thread.Abort` internally — **throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8**. Return an `IActionResult` instead. |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | Return `Redirect(url)` | The `true` (endResponse) parameter triggered `Thread.Abort`. In Core, returning a `RedirectResult` cleanly exits the action. |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | **Remove entirely** | `ThreadAbortException` is never thrown in .NET 8. This catch block was only needed because `Response.Redirect(..., true)` aborted the thread. |
| `Response.AddHeader(...)` | Handled by `FileResult` | Or use `Response.Headers.Append(...)` if you need custom headers. |
| WebForms event handlers (`btnExport_Click`) | Controller action methods with `[HttpPost]` | WebForms postback model doesn't exist in Core. |

## If you have long-running request work

Use `HttpContext.RequestAborted` (a `CancellationToken`) instead of relying on thread aborts for request cancellation:

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> LongRunningExport()
{
    // Pass the token so work stops if the client disconnects
    var data = await GenerateReportAsync(HttpContext.RequestAborted);
    return File(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data), "text/csv", "report.csv");
}
```

## Critical pitfalls to avoid

1. **Don't catch `OperationCanceledException` and swallow it** — let it propagate so ASP.NET Core can handle client disconnects properly.
2. **Don't write to `Response.Body` then return an `IActionResult`** — pick one approach. Mixing them throws `InvalidOperationException`.
3. **`User.Identity` can be `null` in Core** — always use null-conditional (`?.IsAuthenticated`) or check `User.Identity is not null`.
4. **`CancellationTokenSource` is `IDisposable`** — if you create one manually, wrap it in `using`.
